Two crazy new late-game monsters:

J, Yora Hawk, a very tall predatory bird that stands
   erect and has a very long, strong, hooked beak.
   (Source: Aldabreshin Compass series)
   Difficulty: 25+; Base level: 14; Speed: 15;
   Base AC: -5; Base MR: 100; Can fly; Genocidable;
   Weight: 1400; Nutritional Value: 800; Size: Huge;
   Resistant to: fire; Resistances conferred: fire
   Eats: live prey (NOT an engulfer; it kills you first).
   Hunts in groups of 5 + 4d5 individuals.
   Not a valid polymorph target form.
   Attacks: gaze 2d12 (fire), talons 2d24,
            beak 1d60 (grab)

When the Yora Hawk's beak attack hits, it has a 50% chance of
grabbing your body (unless you are insubstantial, e.g., a
ghost), rather like an owlbear only much stronger ("You feel
like the Yora Hawk's powerful beak is breaking your body in
half!")  If you die of this, the death message and tombstone
indicate that the great bird's powerful beak has indeed
snapped you in half like a twig.

The other 50% of the time (when the beak attack hits) it will
grab your primary weapon (if you are wielding one) instead of
your body; when it does this, there is a 1/12 chance it will
break your weapon immediately if non-artifact, 1/60 if it's
an artifact weapon, 1/100 if it's Mjollnir.  If your weapon
is grabbed but not broken, you can still pull away, but your
weapon is pulled from your grasp in the process; you can
recover it by killing the Yora Hawk.  If you do not pull
away, you can only attack the Yora Hawk that has your weapon
(not, for instance, another adjacent Yora Hawk), and it will
not move away from you.  Your attacks in these circumstances
suffer a small to-hit penalty and a significant (25-75%)
damage reduction.  Additionally, there is a further 1/20
chance per turn that your weapon will be broken if
non-artifact, 1/100 if artifact (including Mjollnir).

If you are wielding a stackable weapon, the weapon-grabbing
attack only deprives you of one (each time it happens), not
the whole stack at once.  So if you normally wield Magicbane
but carry a stack of non-artifact throwing daggers, you can
switch to the stack when you see a group of Yora Hawks
approaching and then engage them with relative impunity,
provided your AC and hitpoints and whatnot are buff enough to
let you survive the encounter.  Characters who don't carry
stackable weapons may want to retreat and find some, or at
least a weapon you can afford to lose.

Yora Hawks can be generated theoretically anywhere but are
not especially common.  In a typical game they will not
usually start showing up until after the player has been in
Gehennom, but stat-boosting techniques (e.g., farming) could
possibly allow you to see them somewhat earlier and more
often.  Their frequency should be such that it's not unusual
to go twenty thousand turns without seeing any, but when you
do see them there'll be a group.  They should not occur in
throne rooms or treasure zoos and should never be generated
individually or asleep.


R, Reshaper.  Similar to the disenchanter, but nastier:
   instead of reducing the enchantment of your weapon or
   armor, its attack goes through your inventory, recursively
   through containers, and has a 1/13 chance of reshaping
   EACH item.  Artifacts get their usual chance to resist.

Reshaping is similar to polymorphing but is slightly less
catastrophic: there is no chance of system shock; magic items
are never changed into non-magic ones, nor vice versa; armor
is only changed into something that can fit in the same slot,
e.g., boots will still be boots; one-handed weapons do not
become two-handed once, nor vice versa; stackable weapons do
not become non-stackable ones, and vice versa; containers are
only changed into other containers, and their contents are
not lost; artifacts never become non-artifacts, and vice
versa (and that's after they have their usual chance to
resist).  Note that artifacts still also respect item-type
boundaries; this means certain artifacts CANNOT be changed,
because there is no other artifact in their general item
category, e.g., the only artifact amulets are the Eye of the
Aethiopica and the Amulet of Yendor, and the latter is
obviously exempt, so if the Eye is reshaped it will end up
being the Eye still.  However, an artifact one-handed weapon
could become any artifact one-handed weapon (assuming there
are no stackable artifacts to confuse the issue), and an
artifact tool could become just about any artifact tool.


